he vaccine will be  available to everyone  regardless of status the reality on the  ground is quite different  there's fear and confusion advocates say  the way provinces are approaching  vaccination  excludes and marginalizes migrant  workers and undocumented people  despite being some of the most at risk  they have to make up their minds  if they want to pretend that we're not  here or if they want to include  us in in this in this thing because it's  not discriminative against people like  us it goes after  everyone more than 1.6 million people  in canada are migrants refugees or  undocumented  that's one in every 23 people  they come here through canada's various  temporary foreign worker programs  study permits or without documentation  at all  they work in various frontline  industries such as healthcare  delivery farming construction and in  warehouses  this isn't something that's in the  cracks and the margins in the shadows  and there are significant sectors in our  economy that would grind to a halt and  businesses that would shut down  without undocumented labor while  essential workers have received thanks  and  praise for those with temporary status  or no status at all  the last year has been devastating like  many canadians  migrants and undocumented people have  lost their jobs during this crisis  but unlike most canadians they cannot  access emergency financial aid  they also don't have access to health  care  dr paul callford has been caring for  migrant and undocumented people for more  than two decades  he started this volunteer clinic in  scarborough with some colleagues  after witnessing an undocumented  teenager almost die after being denied  care  we hoped we'd be out of business in one  year after we told everybody what we  found in 1999 and we're still here 23  years later and we're still seeing the  same thing  when the coronavirus spread to canada  paul knew it would be devastating to the  population he treats  in march 2020 paul says six